Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

The shared integer x is initially 0. Three threads execute exactly once:
```text
A: rA = x;  x = 2*rA + 1;
B: rB = x;  x = rB + 3;
C: rC = x;  x = rC + 5;
```
Each displayed read and each displayed write is separately atomic. Local registers are private. Execution is sequentially consistent, and a thread's read precedes its write. After all six operations have completed, how many distinct final values of x are possible?

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

Starting with one process, the following code executes. Every fork succeeds, returns 0 only in its new child, and returns a positive child identifier in its parent. Every process reaches the final line once. How many processes execute work()?

```c
int p = fork();
if (p == 0) fork();
fork();
work();
```

**Required precision:** Integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

A lexically scoped language permits variable shadowing. In the code below, each declaration introduces a distinct variable and declarations take effect immediately. How many distinct declared variables are referenced by the three print expressions, collectively?

```text
var x;
var y;
{
    var x;
    print(x);
    {
        var y;
        print(x + y);
    }
    print(y);
}
```

**Required precision:** Integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

What integer is printed? The static variable is initialized once.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int f(int n) {
    static int k = 0;
    int a = ++k;
    if (n == 0) return a;
    return a + f(n-1);
}
int main(void) {
    int x = f(2);
    int y = f(1);
    printf("%d\n", x+y);
    return 0;
}
```

**Required precision:** Integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

What integer is printed by this conforming C program?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
struct S { int a[2]; int *p; };
int main(void) {
    struct S s = {{1,2}, 0};
    s.p = s.a;
    struct S t = s;
    t.a[0] = 7;
    *t.p = 9;
    printf("%d\n", s.a[0] + t.a[0]);
    return 0;
}
```

**Required precision:** Integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

What integer does this conforming C program print? Each recursive call shares the same pointed-to integer.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int f(int n, int *p) {
    if (n == 0) return *p;
    *p += n;
    int a = f(n-1, p);
    *p -= 1;
    return a + *p;
}
int main(void) {
    int x = 1;
    printf("%d\n", f(3, &x));
    return 0;
}
```

**Required precision:** Integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

What does this conforming C program print?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
void change(int **q, int *r) {
    **q += 2;
    *q = r;
    **q += 3;
}
int main(void) {
    int a = 4, b = 7;
    int *p = &a;
    change(&p, &b);
    printf("%d\n", a + b + *p);
    return 0;
}
```

