Assume conventional C semantics and representable integer values. What is the final value of a[1]?

```c
int a[3] = {1,2,3};
int *p = a;
int x = *p++;
*p += x;
```

**Required precision:** Integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

Assume all array accesses in this C program are valid and int can represent all values shown. What value is returned?

```c
int f(void) {
    int a[4] = {3, 5, 7, 9};
    int *p = a + 1;
    int **q = &p;
    (*q)++;
    **q += a[0];
    return p[-1] + a[2] + (int)(p - a);
}
```

**Required precision:** Exact integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

A loop has these basic blocks; variables are integers and input p is read again at each visit to B1.

```text
B1: p=read(); if p goto B2 else B3;
B2: x=x+1; goto B1;
B3: return x;
```

Using standard backward liveness, how many variables among {p,x} are live at the entry of B1, before p=read()?

**Required precision:** Exact integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

Initially one single-threaded process executes the following pseudocode. Every fork succeeds; a child returns0 from fork, and the parent returns a positive value. No process exits early.

```text
x = fork();
if x == 0 {
    fork();
} else {
    fork();
    fork();
}
```

How many processes, including the original, reach the end?

**Required precision:** Exact integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

Shared x initially equals0. Two threads each execute this sequence once, using the same binary semaphore mutex initially1:

```text
wait(mutex);
t = x;          // t is local to the thread
x = t + 1;
signal(mutex);
```

A third thread performs only wait(mutex); signal(mutex). All three threads finish successfully. How many distinct final values of x are possible?

**Required precision:** Exact integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

What value is printed by this C program? All accesses are within the array.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
void change(int *p) {
    p += 2;
    *p = *(p - 1) + *p;
}
int main(void) {
    int a[4] = {3, 5, 7, 9};
    int *p = a + 1;
    change(p);
    printf("%d", *p + a[3]);
    return 0;
}
```

**Required precision:** Exact integer.

Select ALL options that are NOT valid numerical answers to the following problem. Exactly three options must be selected.

Assume every fork succeeds. Each child starts at the statement after its own fork, and x is ordinary private process memory. How many total lines are printed by all processes?

```c
int x = 0;
if (fork() == 0) x = 1;
if (x == 0) fork();
printf("done\n");
```

No code outside this fragment creates processes or prints lines.

**Required precision:** Exact integer.
